<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Turkish War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1683">1683</a>–<a href="/years/1699">1699</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as War of the Holy League · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Turkish War was fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 1683 to 1699. It began with the failed Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and saw the Habsburgs conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> while Venice took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morean-war">Morean War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 confirmed the first major Ottoman territorial losses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war.jpg" alt="Great Turkish War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DavidDijkgraaf · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Turkish_War_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew out of earlier Ottoman conflicts with Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. In August 1672 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-iv">Mehmed IV</a> attacked Kamenets Podolski, which surrendered, and by the Treaty of Buchach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> ceded Kamenets and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> and agreed to pay tribute. The General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> refused the tribute and raised an army under Sobieski, which won the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotyn">Khotyn</a> in 1673. Sobieski was elected king and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> without success until the Treaty of Żurawno of 17 October 1676, which left the Turks only Kamenets-Podolski.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Ottoman main army laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 14 July 1683. On 6 September the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> under Sobieski joined the Imperial troops and forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, and around six in the evening the allies attacked, with Sobieski leading 3,000 Polish heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-hussars">Winged Hussars</a>. The Ottoman lines broke, and within three hours the Christian forces had won. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> initiated by Pope Innocent XI, joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1684 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1686, then counterattacked.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> since 1541, fell on 2 September 1686 after resisting for 78 days. The Second Battle of Mohács in 1687 was a crushing Ottoman defeat that led to a mutiny, the execution of the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-iv">Mehmed IV</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> by 1688, but when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippsburg">Philippsburg</a> on 27 September 1688 and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, Imperial resources were drawn west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> retook Belgrade in 1690. At the Battle of Slankamen in 1691 Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> defeated the Turks, securing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. Prince Eugene of Savoy won a decisive victory at Zenta on 11 September 1697, killing over 30,000 Ottomans. In the Morean War Francesco Morosini took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Koroni</a> in 1685 and Nafplion in 1686, and by 1687 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> held most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 ended the war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ceded most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>, which the Ottomans regained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> of 1718. The war was the first in which the Ottoman Empire lost substantial territory, and it was the first time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> joined an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. Ottoman sources call it the Disaster Years. After further losses in 1716 to 1718, the Ottomans adopted a more defensive military policy in the following century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Turkish War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Habsburg armies drove the Ottomans out of Slavonia in the <b>Great Turkish War</b>, and the Treaty of Karlowitz in 1699 confirmed the conquest.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-slavonia">Kingdom of Slavonia</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Morean War, part of the <b>Great Turkish War</b>, the Venetian commander Francesco Morosini conquered the Peloponnese between 1685 and 1687; during his campaign in Attica a Venetian shell exploded the Parthenon.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-morea">Venetian Morea</a></li></ul></section>
